Value and Holistic Thinking
1. Focus on Value
According to PMBOK 7, value is the most important measure of project success.

Value can be measured in two ways:

1. Quantitative Value – Value that can be measured using numbers or money.
- Increased income.
- Reduced costs.
- Increased productivity.
2. Qualitative Value – Value that is difficult to measure using numbers.
- Customer satisfaction.
- Good reputation.
- Employee happiness.
How to focus on value in a project?
- Communicate with stakeholders regularly.
- Complete the most valuable tasks first.
- Check the business case regularly.
- Work together with team members.
- Monitor project progress and results.
Example: When developing an online shopping system, completing the payment feature first may provide more value to customers than adding unnecessary design features.
2. Business Case
A Business Case is a document that explains why a project is needed and what value it will provide to the organization.

It helps to decide whether a project is worth doing.

Three main elements of a Business Case:
1. Business Needs – What problem or need should the project solve?
2. Project Justification – Why should the project be started? Is it worth the cost?
3. Business Strategy – How does the project support the organization's goals?
Example:

A company wants to develop an online shopping system.
- Business Needs – Customers need an easy way to buy products online.
- Project Justification – The system can increase sales and reduce manual work.
- Business Strategy – The company wants to expand its online business.

3. Recognize, Evaluate and Respond to System Interactions
System Thinking means understanding how different parts of a system are connected and affect each other.
- A system contains different connected parts.
- Each part can affect other parts.
- A problem in one part can affect the entire system.
- Project managers should consider the whole system, not only individual parts.
- Systems can change over time.
Example: Boat Situation

Imagine a boat has a hole and water enters it.
- Two people sit on one side to balance the boat.
- Two other people remove the water.
- Everyone works together to keep the boat safe.
This shows system thinking because everyone works together to solve one problem.







